     Monetary transmission into NEPSE runs through two parallel channels operating on different timelines: a slower fundamentals channel (falling rates improving bank margins conditionally, hydropower debt servicing, and corporate earnings generally, over two to four quarters) and a faster liquidity-rotation channel (falling deposit rates and cheaper margin financing pulling household savings directly into equities, often within weeks of a rate cut). NEPSE rallies driven predominantly by the second channel, without confirmation from the first, are structurally more fragile and prone to sharp reversal if liquidity conditions tighten again.
